// The section "null, unions and narrowing" of the English guide; guide.rk lists the sections.

import { Example } from "../../example.rk"

export comp NullSafety() {
    return <>
        <p>
            A value of type <code>?T</code> may be <code>null</code>, and a value of type
            {" "}<code>T</code> may not. The fields of a <code>?T</code> cannot be used without a
            check: the error comes at compile time, not in the user's browser.
        </p>
        <p>
            After <code>if todo != null</code> the type narrows to <code>Todo</code>. The familiar
            {" "}<code>?.</code> and <code>??</code> work with <code>?T</code> too.
        </p>
        <p>
            Where the program knows more than the compiler, <code>todo!</code> takes a value not
            to be <code>null</code>, as in TypeScript; it is not checked at runtime. A function
            whose result is <code>never</code>, one that throws or exits as
            {" "}<code>process.exit</code>, does not return, so the code after its call is
            narrowed as after <code>throw</code>.
        </p>
        <Example name="null-safety" />
        <p>
            A type can be a union of several: <code>string | number</code>. The literal type
            {" "}<code>"all"</code> allows only that value, so
            {" "}<code>{'type Filter "all" | "active" | "done"'}</code> catches typos in strings.
            Unions narrow like <code>?T</code>: with <code>typeof</code>, <code>instanceof</code>, a
            comparison with a literal and <code>switch</code>.
        </p>
        <Example name="unions" />
        <p>
            Messages, events and API responses are often one of several kinds, told apart by a
            field like <code>kind</code>: a tagged union. Comparing the tag narrows the value, so
            after <code>{'message.kind == "photo"'}</code> the fields of a photo can be read. An
            object literal chooses its member by the tag too, and a kind that does not exist is an
            error. Paths of fields narrow like variables, without an intermediate
            {" "}<code>const</code>: after <code>message.caption != null</code> the caption is a
            {" "}<code>string</code>, and after <code>{'order.delivery.kind == "courier"'}</code>
            {" "}<code>order.delivery</code> is the courier's delivery.
        </p>
        <Example name="tagged-unions" />
    </>
}
